WEBVTT

cue-VID_U5_01-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Meet Northwater Kayaks, a small firm making sea kayaks in a converted garage. Demand is rising, but the owner cannot build more boats with cash alone. Finance is money or capital needed to pay for expenditure and business operations. Without it, orders wait, stock runs short and growth stalls. Watch the finance fountain fill the business, one source at a time, right now, before the summer season.

cue-VID_U5_01-2
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Businesses need finance for five clear reasons. Start-up capital pays for essential assets before trading begins. Expansion funds new premises and bigger output. Replacing worn non-current assets keeps production running. New technology raises efficiency and lowers unit costs. Working capital pays day-to-day bills. Match the time period to the source: stock and monthly bills are short-term, repaid within one year; machinery and factory expansion are long-term, repaid over many years.

cue-VID_U5_01-3
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Here is Northwater's concrete plan. A new moulding machine costs fifty thousand dollars and will last five years. Extra timber and fabric stock costs twelve thousand dollars and sells within three months. The total need is sixty-two thousand dollars. Because one machine is long-term and the stock is short-term, a single source cannot sensibly fund both. So the finance fountain must divide into two separate streams of different lengths.

cue-VID_U5_01-4
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Internal finance comes from within the business or its owners. Three tiles drop into the fountain: ten thousand dollars of the owner's savings, twelve thousand dollars of retained profit, and three thousand dollars from selling an old trailer. That gives twenty-five thousand dollars. Internal finance avoids interest payments and keeps control with the owner. The limit is size: savings are finite, and spending retained profit leaves less cash for emergencies.

cue-VID_U5_01-5
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:28.000
External finance comes from sources outside the business. A five-year bank loan supplies twenty-five thousand dollars for the machine. A twelve-thousand-dollar overdraft covers stock for three months. A share issue, a government grant or trade credit are other external tiles. External sources raise large sums quickly, but loans charge interest and must be repaid whether profit rises or falls. An overdraft suits a temporary gap, never a ten-year factory expansion.

cue-VID_U5_01-6
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Compare debt with equity on the split screen. A bank loan keeps ownership with the founder, and interest is predictable. But repayments continue when sales fall, and the machine may be used as security. Issuing shares brings cash with no fixed repayment or interest, and investors share the risk. The cost is dilution: existing owners give up a slice of control and future dividends. Neither source is free; the right choice depends on duration, cost and legal form.

cue-VID_U5_01-7
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Put the sources on a timeline. In months one to three, the overdraft finances stock and is repaid as kayaks sell. Across years one to five, the bank loan pays for the moulding machine as it earns revenue. Because the stock cycle is short and the machine is long-lived, therefore each source is matched to its purpose, so the business lowers its refinancing risk. The internal twenty-five thousand dollars reduces borrowing, and retained profit stays as a cushion.

cue-VID_U5_01-8
00:03:28.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Before choosing, a finance manager checks seven factors: business size, legal form, amount needed, length of time, existing loans, cost and purpose. A sole trader cannot sell shares to the public, so internal cash or a bank loan is realistic. A larger company can issue equity but gives up control. Advantages sit beside limitations: speed against interest, control against dilution, flexibility against repayment. Finance keeps a kayak business afloat only when the source fits the need.

